<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#canadian-red-ensign">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Canadian Red Ensign</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Canadian Red Ensign was a red flag with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-flag">Union Jack</a> in the canton and the Canadian coat of arms in the fly, used as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>'s unofficial and later official national flag on land and at sea from the late nineteenth century. Canadian forces fought under it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. It was replaced by the Maple Leaf flag on 15 February 1965.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/canadian-red-ensign.jpg" alt="Canadian Red Ensign" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Denelson83 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Canada_(1957%E2%80%931965).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>A Red Ensign bearing a Canadian emblem was used on land and at sea from as early as 1868, soon after Confederation, on an informal basis. Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-a-macdonald">Sir John A. Macdonald</a> constantly made use of it, and an 1891 memo of the Governor General, Lord Stanley, noted that the Dominion Government encouraged its use on public buildings. In 1892 an Admiralty warrant authorised it for ships registered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, which the Canada Shipping Act of 1934 confirmed. The original shield bore the arms of the four founding provinces, and flag makers often added wreaths and crowns or enlarged the badge as provinces joined.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The ensign flew over the Parliament Buildings until 1904, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-flag">Union Flag</a> replaced it, and it featured in recruiting and patriotic displays in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. The flag carried by the 5th Canadian Infantry Battalion at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vimy-ridge">Battle of Vimy Ridge</a> in 1917 survives and is possibly the oldest Canadian flag in existence. It was given to the Imperial War Museum, which lent it to the new Canadian War Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottawa">Ottawa</a> in 2005 and took it back in 2008.</p><p>On 26 April 1922 an order-in-council placed the new shield of the arms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, designed by the College of Arms, on the ensign; its maple leaves were green until 1957. In 1924 the flag was approved for government buildings outside Canada, and on 5 September 1945 Order-in-Council P.C. 5888 named it a distinctive Canadian flag for federal buildings within or outside Canada, so that it once again flew over Parliament.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the Great Flag Debate the Maple Leaf flag was adopted in 1965, but the ensign kept broad sympathy, and the provincial flags adopted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> in 1965 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba">Manitoba</a> in 1966 are red ensigns. In 2007 the Public Register of Arms, Flags and Badges of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> recognised it as a national symbol of Canada. It flies beside the Maple Leaf at war memorials such as the Canadian National Vimy Memorial and is part of the Royal Canadian Legion's colour party. In the 2010s some far-right and white nationalist groups adopted it, which drew protests from the Legion and others.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Canadian Red Ensign” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…term dominion fell out of official use after the Second World War, the Maple Leaf flag replaced the <b>Red Ensign</b> in 1965, and the patriation of the constitution in 1982 removed the last legal power of the British Parliament over Canada, although Quebec’s government never endorsed the settlement.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>The red and white Maple Leaf flag was raised on Parliament Hill in Ottawa, replacing the <b>Canadian Red Ensign</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-15">15 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
